<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aulus Plautius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aulus Plautius was a Roman senator and general who commanded the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> ordered by the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> in 43 CE. Leading four legions, he defeated the Catuvellauni, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camulodunum">Camulodunum</a> with the emperor present, and accepted the surrender of peoples such as part of the Dobunni. He served as the first governor of the province of Britannia until 47 and was honoured with an ovation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aulus-plautius.jpg" alt="Aulus Plautius" width="320" height="264" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Piotr Stachiewicz (1858-1938) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stachiewicz-Aulus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of the early career of Aulus Plautius. He was the son of Aulus Plautius, suffect consul in 1 BCE, and his younger brother Quintus Plautius was consul in 36. An inscription once thought to record him in the suppression of a slave revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> is now identified as naming his father. Aulus himself was suffect consul for the second half of 29, with Lucius Nonius Asprenas as his colleague. He then held a provincial governorship, probably of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>, early in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>, and an inscription shows that he oversaw the building of a road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rijeka">Rijeka</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> appointed Plautius to lead the invasion of Britannia in 43. The stated cause was to restore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verica">Verica</a>, king of the Atrebates and an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, who had been deposed by the Catuvellauni. Anthony Birley argues that Plautius was chosen out of gratitude for his loyalty the previous year, when he may have helped suppress the coup of Lucius Arruntius Camillus Scribonianus, and for his family connections to the imperial house. The force comprised four legions, IX Hispana, II Augusta, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legio-xiv-gemina">XIV Gemina</a> and XX Valeria Victrix, with about 20,000 auxiliaries. The future emperor Vespasian commanded II Augusta.</p><p>On the beaches of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> the troops mutinied, unwilling to cross the Ocean and fight beyond the limits of the known world. They were persuaded after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>'s freedman Narcissus addressed them; seeing a former slave in place of their commander, they cried &quot;Io <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saturnalia">Saturnalia</a>!&quot; and the mutiny ended. The army is generally believed to have landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richborough">Richborough</a> in Kent. The Britons under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togodumnus">Togodumnus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caratacus">Caratacus</a> avoided pitched battles, but Plautius defeated Caratacus on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-medway">River Medway</a> and Togodumnus on the Thames. He then halted and sent for Claudius, who arrived with elephants and heavy artillery and completed the march on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camulodunum">Camulodunum</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colchester">Colchester</a>).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In the following years the legions widened the new province: Legio IX advanced north along <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ermine-street">Ermine Street</a> to a camp at what became Lincoln, Legio XIV into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a> and then to what became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a>, and Legio II under Vespasian across the south, reducing over 20 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-fort">hill forts</a> and conquering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isle-of-wight">Isle of Wight</a>. Plautius was replaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-ostorius-scapula">Publius Ostorius Scapula</a> and on his return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> was granted an ovation, during which the emperor himself walked beside him to and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitol</a>. His wife Pomponia Graecina was charged in 57 with a &quot;foreign superstition&quot;, which some interpret as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, was tried by her husband before her kinsmen and was acquitted.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aulus Plautius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 43 CE the emperor Claudius, seeking military prestige, sent <b>Aulus Plautius</b> with four legions, the II Augusta, IX Hispana, XIV Gemina and XX, and auxiliaries, perhaps 40,000 men in all.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 43 part of the Dobunni surrendered to <b>Aulus Plautius</b>, and the region came under Roman control without major recorded conflict.</blockquote><a href="/state/dobunni">Dobunni</a></li></ul></section>
